# govulncheck waiver policy (spec §15, release pipeline step 4)
#
# NOTHING READS THIS FILE. Adding an entry here suppresses no finding.
#
# Both `.github/workflows/govulncheck.yml` and the `govulncheck` job in
# `.github/workflows/release.yml` run plain `govulncheck ./...`. There is no
# `-json` parse step and no ID subtraction, so the gate fails on any reachable
# vulnerability and no file edit can waive one. `release.yml` declares
# `needs: [govulncheck, docs-live]`, so a failure blocks the tag.
#
# Waiving a finding therefore requires a change to the workflow itself, in a
# reviewed PR. A waiver added that way MUST meet all four conditions below.
#
#   1. The vulnerability is in a transitive dependency with no patched version,
#      or the reachable function is proven unreachable at our use site, with a
#      link to the audit.
#   2. The waiver carries a reviewer name and an expiry date, 90 days maximum.
#   3. An expired waiver is a hard failure. Re-evaluate before extending it.
#   4. The release notes for the tag link the waiver diff, so users see what
#      was waived.
#
# The file is kept as the written standard for that review. If a suppression
# path is ever wired, wire it to a format declared here and delete this notice.
